      "content": "That makes sense in a theoretical sense, but it feels like you're creating a bit of a loophole there. If \"prudence\" and \"respect for prerogatives\" are left up to the discretion of the political actors, then the \"balance\" you're talking about is essentially just a polite way of saying the executive gets to decide when it matters and when it doesn't.\n\nIn practice, when a legislature tries to exercise oversight that the President deems \"unduly constraining,\" the outcome is almost always a constitutional crisis. Given that reality, isn't it more legally accurate to say that the \"Executive Primacy\" position is actually the only one that provides a stable, predictable rule of law, whereas the \"Oversight\" position just leads to constitutional gridlock and instability? I'm trying to see if the primacy argument is actually the more \"functional\" legal necessity for a modern state, rather than just a preference for power.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
